Environment, alignment, handling, and the specimen itself (@met-environment: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @metrology, #source "../sources/Dimensional-Metrology.lingua#r_5XRAV7WQWR6CTM94WM04G0Z647", #source "../sources/GUM-Models.lingua#r_3AAJPGTHAAF72R05SJ1VJ7HN46", #metrology "Instrument-response.lingua#r_07SCGP3DGSTJ27T288T3G50J8H") { r_1RNJYM0X929ZYV4TKHKDA5FTQC
**Read:** [Fundamental Good Practice in Dimensional Metrology — Flack and Hannaford](../sources/Dimensional-Metrology.lingua#r_5XRAV7WQWR6CTM94WM04G0Z647), the complete guide and its physical error discussions; [Developing and using measurement models — JCGM GUM-6](../sources/GUM-Models.lingua#r_3AAJPGTHAAF72R05SJ1VJ7HN46), clauses 9–10 on effects and model extensions.

The measuring system includes the object and its interaction with references, fixtures, people, and surroundings. Temperature, humidity, pressure, vibration, air currents, cleanliness, mounting force, warm-up, and elapsed time can affect different quantities in different ways. State which influences matter to the intended uncertainty and how they are controlled, observed, corrected, or bounded.

For a teaching steel length of 100 mm, assumed expansion coefficient α = 11.5 × 10^−6 K^−1, and temperature 5 K above the reference, the first-order expansion is LαΔT = 0.00575 mm, or 5.75 µm. This is already large relative to some dimensional claims. The coefficient, temperature distribution, reference convention, and model also have uncertainty. Using a familiar nominal coefficient without identifying the material is an assumption.

Geometry can create systematic effects. A projection L cos θ differs from L; near zero angle, its relative difference is approximately −θ²/2. An offset between the measurement axis and the defined length can introduce a first-order angular error, often discussed through the Abbe principle. Contact force can deform the specimen or probe rather than merely reveal a pre-existing distance.

**Work:** write an influence map for an ordinary dimensional comparison. Rank likely effects using rough numerical estimates, and describe an alignment or reversal experiment that could distinguish two of them. Design a stabilization observation rather than assuming a waiting time guarantees equilibrium.

Do not inflate a budget with every conceivable influence while omitting the dominant one. Explain why an effect is negligible relative to the purpose, or retain it with an appropriate model. Later instrument-family studies return to this physical judgement at a more demanding scale.

